  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Mr Smith’s current age is exactly twice his wife’s age when he was six years older than she is now. Their combined current age is 53. How old are they?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Mr Smith is 32 years old and Mrs Smith is 21 years old.\n\nUse the same elapsed time for both ages to form simultaneous equations.",
      "worked": "Let $S$ and $W$ be Mr and Mrs Smith’s current ages in years. When Mr Smith was $W+6$ years old, the elapsed time was $S-(W+6)$ years, so his wife’s age then was $W-[S-(W+6)]$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S+W&=53\\\\S&=2\\bigl(W-[S-(W+6)]\\bigr)\\\\S&=4W-2S+12\\\\3S&=4W+12\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitute $S=53-W$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3(53-W)&=4W+12\\\\159-3W&=4W+12\\\\147&=7W\\\\W&=21\\\\S&=53-21\\\\S&=32\\end{align*}$$\n\nMr Smith is 32 years old and Mrs Smith is 21 years old. Five years ago their ages were 27 and 16: $27=21+6$ and $32=2(16)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 132
  },
